INT. SMOKEHOUSE KITCHEN - ICELANDIC FISHING VILLAGE - NIGHT

Tin walls. Hooks of cod hang in blue smoke. A woodstove ticks.

HELGA SIGURDARDOTTIR, 40s, rubber apron, forearms scarred from knives, slices a split haddock. Fast, precise.

BJARNI, 16, headphones around his neck, stands in the doorway with a duffel bag. He has been standing there a while.

Helga doesn't look up.

HELGA
Shut the door. You're letting out the smoke.

He steps in. Closes it. Doesn't put down the bag.

BJARNI
I got into the school.

HELGA
In Reykjavik.

BJARNI
You knew?

HELGA
The letter was on the table.

BJARNI
You read my letter.

HELGA
It was on the table.

She wipes the knife. Lays it down, parallel to the board.

HELGA (CONT'D)
When's the bus?

BJARNI
Six.

HELGA
In the morning?

BJARNI
Tonight.

She turns. Looks at the duffel. Looks at him.

HELGA
You were going to leave without telling me.

BJARNI
I'm telling you.

HELGA
After you packed.

He shrugs. She picks up a fish and slaps it on the board. Cuts. Cuts again. He winces at each blow.

BJARNI
Mamma.

HELGA
Hand me the salt.

He sets down the duffel at last. Crosses to the shelf. Brings a tin of coarse salt. She takes it without a word and pours a ridge down the haddock's spine.

BJARNI
Dad would've said go.

HELGA
Your father would've said go and then not paid for it.

BJARNI
That's not fair.

HELGA
It's smoked fish. Nothing is fair.

A silence. Smoke drifts between them. Helga glances up through it, and for a moment her face almost opens.

The back door scrapes. Cold air, a flurry of snow.

ASGEIR THORVALDSSON, 80, her father-in-law, enormous in a wool coat and a seal-fur hat, shoulders in carrying a crate of frozen herring.

ASGEIR
Who died?

HELGA
Nobody, Asgeir.

ASGEIR
Then why do you both look like funerals?

He sets the crate on the table with a crash. Notices the duffel. Notices Bjarni. Understands.

ASGEIR (CONT'D)
Ah.

He sits on a stool. Takes off his hat. Rubs his bald head.

ASGEIR (CONT'D)
When?

BJARNI
Tonight.

ASGEIR
Good. A boy should leave in the dark. Less to look back at.

HELGA
Don't encourage him.

ASGEIR
He has your jaw, Helga. He'll go whether I encourage or no.

Helga laughs, short, and turns her back to hide it. She scrubs her knife at the sink. Bjarni steps toward her, hand half-raised.

Asgeir stands, pulls the boy into a crushing hug that lifts him off the floor. Bjarni yelps. Helga turns, drying her hands, fighting a smile. The boy squirms, red-faced, and Asgeir finally sets him down. He cuffs the back of Bjarni's head, tenderly.

ASGEIR (CONT'D)
Go. Take the herring.

BJARNI
It's frozen.

ASGEIR
It travels well.

Helga takes a paper bag from a drawer and begins to wrap smoked haddock in it. Her hands are steady. Her eyes are not.

HELGA
Six fillets. And the dried fish for the bus.

BJARNI
It stinks.

HELGA
That's how you'll find your way home.

He takes the parcel. Holds it with both hands. For a long moment the three of them stand in the blue smoke, none of them moving.

Then Bjarni picks up the duffel. Crosses to the door. Stops with his hand on the latch.

BJARNI
Mamma. I'll call Sundays.

HELGA
Call when you've got something to say.

BJARNI
Sundays, then.

He goes. The door shuts. Snow swirls in the gap and settles on the floor.

Asgeir takes a stool. Helga returns to the haddock.

ASGEIR
He'll be fine.

HELGA
He'll eat badly.

ASGEIR
So did his father.

She sniffs. Presses the knife into the fish. Cuts clean through.

She stops. Sets the knife down. Her shoulders drop, just once.

Asgeir reaches across the table and pats her hand with a fish-scented glove.
